hi this is pressure Locker let's go over  four methods to subtract numbers that  are part of the common core curriculum  one method is subtracting numbers from  right to left this is the traditional  method let's say you want to do 360  minus 156 we start from the far right  and we see if we can take away 6 from 0  since 6 is larger than 0 we need to  carry over so this 6 from the tens  column becomes a 5 and the 0 becomes a  10 now we subtract column by column 10  minus 6 is 4 5 minus 5 is 0 and 3 minus  1 is 2 so we have 204 another method is  to subtract numbers left to right this  is very good for mental math so let's  say you want to do the same problem we  subtract the first digit which is 1 so  we have 360 minus 100 in our head we  store 260 then we need to subtract 50  which gets us to 210 and then finally we  subtract 6 which gets us to 204 so this  looks complicated but it can be good for  mental math a third method is known as  Singapore and you don't need to carry  over the numbers so let's say we want to  do 360 minus 156 this 6 is greater than  a 0 so to avoid this we'll subtract 6  from each number so from 360 we're going  to subtract 6 which gets us 354 and from  156 we also subtract 6 which gets us to  150 now to do this problem we can  subtract column by column so 4 minus 0  will be 4 5 minus 5 is 0 and 3 minus 1  is 2 so the great thing about the  Singapore method is you don't have to  carry over a final method is known as  counting up and this is often good for  if you want to give change so let's say  you buy an item costing off dollar 56  and you give 3 dollars and 60 cents  how will they know how much change to  give well first they can give you four  cents which brings your total two  hundred ones dollar sixty cents then  they might give you forty cents which  brings you to two dollars then they  might give you a dollar which brings the  total to three dollars and finally  they'll give you another sixty cents  which gets us to 360 so how much change  has the cash you're giving you in total  we add up the blue numbers so four plus  40 plus the dollar plus 60 is two  dollars and four cents I hope you liked  this video please subscribe to my  channel you can support me on patreon  catch me on my blog mind your decisions  on Twitter at pressure Locker and you  can get my books at Amazon
 

